With regard to the etymology of this word, {{zh-ref|Huang (1980)}}, {{zh-ref|Norman (1983)}} and {{zh-ref|Zhou (1986)}} hypothesised that this is the same as {{och-l|農|farmer; peasant}}. The use of this word as a pronoun may have originated as a form of personal deprecation and then come to be used as a full-fledged pronoun. {{zh-ref|Pan and Chen (1995)}} disagree, proposing that ''nong'' was originally a {{w|Baiyue}} substrate word possibly of {{der|zh|qfa-tak}} origin, and possibly an original clan name later developing to mean “person; I”. Compare {{cog|za|Nungz|pos=surname}} and name of the 11<sup>th</sup> century [[Zhuang]] leader {{w|Nong Zhigao}}, as well as the name of the {{w|Nùng}} people in Vietnam.

