[[File:Antique_Japanese_(samurai)_hari_bachi_kabuto.jpg|thumb|250px|{{m|ja|兜|tr=kabuto}}: a traditional Japanese '''[[helmet]]'''.]]

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=かぶと|-}}, from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|sort=かぶと|*kanputo}}. Cognate with {{cog|ryu|兜|tr=kantu}}. Attested in the ''{{w|Shin'yaku Kegonkyō Ongi Shiki}}'' (794) with the spellings {{lang|ja|可夫刀}} and {{lang|ja|可夫度}}.<ref name="JDB">{{R:JDB|page=213}}</ref> One source cites a manuscript of the ''{{w|Nihon Shoki}}'' (720) in which the word {{zh-l|冑|tr=-}} in the word has a katakana gloss {{lang|ja|カフト}} but these glosses may be later readings.<ref name="KDJ2O"/>

Derivation currently {{unk|ja|sort=かぶと|nocap=1}}.
* A surface analysis might suggest a derivation from {{m|ja|被る|tr=kaburu||to wear something on the head}}. However, that reading derives from older form ''kagafuru'' and does not appear until the ''{{w|Nihon Ryōiki}}'' (810-824),<ref name="JDB-kaburu">{{R:JDB|page=213}}</ref> some time after the first appearance of ''kabuto''.
* An alternative analysis might suggest a compound of {{compound|ja|sort=かぶと|頭|tr1=kabu|t1=head|pos1=''[[kun'yomi]]'' and native Japanese term|兜|tr2=to|t2=helmet|pos2=''[[on'yomi]]'' and borrowing from Chinese}}.
* Word-medial bilabial plosives usually underwent [[lenition]], shifting along the lines of {{IPAchar|/p/}} → {{IPAchar|/f/}} → {{IPAchar|/w/}}, then vanishing altogether except where the following vowel was {{IPAchar|/a/}}. This lenition often did not happen at morpheme boundaries in compound words. The persistence of the {{IPAchar|/b/}} in ''kabuto'' might thus suggest that this term was originally a compound of ''ka'' + ''puto''. The ''ka'' element is uncertain, possibly the {{ja-r|か-}} intensifying prefix added to adjectives, or an abbreviation of {{m|ja|髪|tr=kami|t=hair (of the head)}} or {{m|ja|上|tr=kami|t=top}} (compare {{m|ja|挿頭す|tr=kazasu|t=to put decorations onto a hair or crown|pos=possibly from ''kami'' + {{m|ja|刺す|tr=sasu|t=to poke}}}}; {{cog|ojp|-}} ''puto'' would be the stem and root of modern {{m|ja|太い|tr=futoi||[[thick]]; [[fat]]; [[stout]]}}, possibly in reference to the protective strength provided by a helmet. This ''puto'' would then have undergone {{ja-rendaku}} to become ''buto''.
: Compare the phonology of adjective {{m|ja|か細い|tr=kabosoi||very slender}}, composed of this ''ka-'' prefix and adjective {{m|ja|細い|tr=hosoi|pos=ancient ''pososi''}} and demonstrating a similar retention of the bilabial plosive and {{ja-rendaku}}.

