===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
[[File:Yanzhou Museum 2015.08.13 15-15-43.jpg|thumb|100px|A {{zh-l|*瓮}} from the {{w|Longshan culture}} (also called Black Pottery Culture) {{defdate|3000 – 1900 BC}}.]]

The traditional interpretation as given by ''{{w|Han Feizi}}'' is that {{lang|zh|公}} is a compound of {{zh-l|*八}} (= {{zh-l|*背|to deviate; opposite}}) and {{zh-l|*厶}} (original form of {{zh-l|*私|individual; private}}), i.e. the opposite of “private” — “public”. This theory is supported by Sun Yirang and Qiang Kaiyun, despite the somewhat different shape of the bottom component from {{lang|zh|厶}}.

This is disputed in modern times by Gao Hongjin, who thinks that the {{lang|zh|八}} on top stands for {{zh-l|*分|to divide; to distribute}} and the bottom round component {{zh-l|*口}} symbolises a generic object. {{lang|zh|公}} thus represents “equally dividing resources so that they are '''communal'''”.

Another theory put forth by Zhu Fangpu is that {{lang|zh|公}} is a {{liushu|p|nocap=1}}, being the original form of {{och-l|瓮|a wide-mouthed round-bottomed [[jar]]}}, and later borrowed for the meanings of “public” and “male of older generation or higher rank”.

===Etymology===
; "fair; impartial; public"
: From {{der|zh|sit}}; cognate with {{cog|bo|གུང}}, {{m|bo|དགུང||middle}}, considering that "middle" may be semantically associated with "balance" ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). {{attn|zh|STEDT: *(t/d)uŋ ⪤ *(t/d)waŋ MIDDLE / NAVEL?}}

; "male of older generation or higher rank; duke; male (of animals)"
: This word has been connected to {{och-l|翁|tr= *qˤoŋ}} ({{zh-ref|Wang, 1982}}; {{zh-ref|Unger, 1999}}; {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart, 2017}}). {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2017)}} also includes {{och-l|妐|husband's father|tr= *t-qoŋ}} in this word family.
: {{w|Sino-Tibetan Etymological Dictionary and Thesaurus|STEDT}} provisionally sets up {{cog|sit-pro||*kaŋ|father; grandfather; honorific}}, also comparing it to {{zh-l|*翁}}; compare {{cog|duu|kangkang||grandpa}}, {{m|duu|vkang||(my/our) grandfather}}, {{cog|my|ခင်}}, as in {{m|my|မိခင်||mother}}, {{m|my|ဖခင်||father}}. Alternatively, STEDT also compares it with {{cog|sit-pro|*gaŋ||penis; male}}, whence {{och-l|雄|male}}, but considers it less probable.
: {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} suggests a {{der|zh|mkh}} origin; compare {{cog|km|ឡូញ||title of an unidentified rank or function}} and {{cog|okz|khloñ||head, chief}} (whence {{m|km|ខ្លោង}}). Compare also {{cog|th|ลุง}}, from {{cog|tai-pro|*luŋᴬ||parent's older brother}}, which may also be from {{cog|mkh}}. {{cog|bo|ཁོང}} ~ {{m|bo|གོང|t=final syllable in dignitaries' name}} may be borrowed from Chinese.

