===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|えん|yomi=o}}
{{swp|ja:}}
{{swp|ja:円 (数学)}}
{{swp|ja:円 (通貨)}}
{{IPAchar|/wen/}} → {{IPAchar|/eɴ/}}

From {{der|ja|ltc|sort=えん|-}} {{ltc-l|圓|id=2}}, with the original sense of "[[circle]], [[round]]ness".

The ''[[yen]]'' sense came much later in the {{w|Meiji period}}, from {{bor|ja|zh|sort=えん|圓|tr=yuán}}, itself from {{m|zh|銀圓|tr=yínyuán||round [[silver#Adjective|silver]] object(s), ''especially'' a [[piece of eight]]}}. Compare {{cog|zh|元|tr=yuán||[[yuan]]}} and {{cog|ko|원||Korean [[won]]}}. {{doublet|ja|元|ユアン|ウォン|tr1=gen|t1=[[yuan]] {{gloss|Chinese currency}}|tr2=yuan|t2=[[yuan]] {{gloss|Chinese currency}}|tr3=won|t3=[[won]] {{gloss|Korean currency}}}}.

The ''yen'' became the official currency of Japan in 1871, succeeding the {{ja-r|両|りょう}} and the {{ja-r|文|もん}}.

